    "q-80524": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80524",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{2+2}{1}=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
