    "q-140470": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140470",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}(2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}).$\n So, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}) \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-140470-a",
